 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/find-the-duplicate-number/",
 "pattern": "Cycle detection on index → value links",
 "statement": "`nums` holds n + 1 integers, each between 1 and n, so at least one value must repeat. Exactly one value is repeated (possibly several times). Find it **without modifying the array** and using only constant extra space.",
 "example": {
  "input": "nums = [1,3,4,2,2]",
  "output": "2",
  "why": "The value 2 appears at index 3 and at index 4."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "nums = [3,1,3,4,2]",
   "output": "3"
  },
  {
   "input": "nums = [1,1]",
   "output": "1"
  },
  {
   "input": "nums = [2,2,2,2,2]",
   "output": "2"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Read every value as a pointer: from index `i`, go to index `nums[i]`. Starting at index 0 this traces a path.",
  "Two indexes hold the duplicate value, so two arrows lead to the same place. That makes the path loop — and the **entrance of the loop is the duplicate**.",
  "Find the loop with slow/fast pointers (Floyd's algorithm). Then restart one pointer from the beginning and move both one hop at a time: they meet exactly at the entrance."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def findDuplicate(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        slow = nums[0]\n        fast = nums[0]\n\n        # Move slow and fast pointers\n        while True:\n            slow = nums[slow]\n            fast = nums[nums[fast]]\n            if slow == fast:\n                break\n\n        # Find the entrance of the cycle\n        slow = nums[0]\n        while slow != fast:\n            slow = nums[slow]\n            fast = nums[fast]\n\n        return slow",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n)",
  "timeWhy": "Both phases walk at most a few times around the path.",
  "space": "O(1)",
  "spaceWhy": "Two index variables; the array is never changed."
 },
